  <entry>
    <id>tag:github.com,2008:Grit::Commit/59afd594f8bcbba9caa30d908462122818178d7b</id>
    <link type="text/html" rel="alternate" href="https://github.com/serjflint/saitenka/commit/59afd594f8bcbba9caa30d908462122818178d7b"/>
    <title>
        fix(subtitles): key the run-path cache and picker picks by profile la…
    </title>
    <updated>2026-09-06T19:25:39Z</updated>
    <media:thumbnail height="30" width="30" url="https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/4900246?s=30&amp;v=4"/>
    <author>
      <name>serjflint</name>
      <uri>https://github.com/serjflint</uri>
    </author>
    <content type="html">
      &lt;pre style=&#39;white-space:pre-wrap;width:81ex&#39;&gt;fix(subtitles): key the run-path cache and picker picks by profile language

Two paths kept writing or reading the unmarked (Japanese) cache slot after
TsukiHime stopped being Japanese-only, each reopening the cross-profile hijack
the language-keyed slot was added to close.

`launch.run._configured_subtitles` gated the cache read on &amp;quot;some provider serves
this language&amp;quot; and then read the slot with no language. That gate was sufficient
only while every provider was Japanese-only; widening TsukiHime made it true for
every language, so a French run was served the Japanese file a prior run had
cached. Nothing covered `_configured_subtitles`, so the gap was invisible.

The picker stored a pick under the attachment&amp;#39;s own tag. For an untagged
attachment — the case the boundary deliberately lets through — that resolved to
the Japanese slot, orphaning the hand pick for the profile that made it and
serving it to a Japanese one. Picks are keyed by the profile that chose them;
the boundary has already established the pick belongs to it.

Also: the picker&amp;#39;s mixed-language test compared display names, so one language
spelled two ways (fr/fra) labelled every row; it compares language bases now.
A test comment claimed `fra` as a negative control for `fr` when it is a valid
alias that matches — the case is now present and asserts what it does.&lt;/pre&gt;
    </content>
  </entry>
  <entry>
    <id>tag:github.com,2008:Grit::Commit/998c063270e8a198adf3e5b320c1c23f06820573</id>
    <link type="text/html" rel="alternate" href="https://github.com/serjflint/saitenka/commit/998c063270e8a198adf3e5b320c1c23f06820573"/>
    <title>
        feat(subtitles): serve every language TsukiHime carries, keyed by pro…
    </title>
    <updated>2026-09-06T18:52:41Z</updated>
    <media:thumbnail height="30" width="30" url="https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/4900246?s=30&amp;v=4"/>
    <author>
      <name>serjflint</name>
      <uri>https://github.com/serjflint</uri>
    </author>
    <content type="html">
      &lt;pre style=&#39;white-space:pre-wrap;width:81ex&#39;&gt;feat(subtitles): serve every language TsukiHime carries, keyed by profile

The provider dropped every non-Japanese attachment while parsing a release
(`_is_japanese` in `_attachment_from_raw`). A census of 30 releases found 30+
languages, with French attachments outnumbering Japanese ones — so the filter
discarded most of what the source serves, and &amp;quot;TsukiHime as a French source&amp;quot;
was a filter to delete rather than an adapter to write.

Language becomes part of the provider contract: candidates carry their raw
reported tag, `ProviderContext` carries the profile&amp;#39;s target, and the boundary
applies the existing `lang_matches` policy to a non-blank tag. An untagged
result is not auto-attached — it reaches the manual picker instead, since a
human can judge what a missing tag cannot.

Three things had to move with it:

- The filter runs before `fetch`&amp;#39;s uniqueness guard. A release holds one
  subtitle per language, so counting all of them made every real multilingual
  release ambiguous.
- The cache slot gains a language. It was keyed on (video, title, episode,
  size, mode), so a French session could be served the Japanese file a previous
  session stored; the jp-only capability had been the accidental guard.
  Japanese keeps the unmarked legacy spelling, so existing hand-picked `-raw`
  and `-retimed` entries stay addressable.
- `ProviderContext.language` defaults to the target rather than to None, which
  means &amp;quot;do not filter&amp;quot; — the wrong default for an unattended fetch.

`fetch_provider_path` takes the context whole instead of unpacking seven
keywords and repacking them one line later.&lt;/pre&gt;
    </content>
  </entry>
